passages.english:
At one time all things coming together into one by Love,
at another again each borne apart by the hatred of Strife,
and again when the one has grown apart they become many;
in this way they come to be and have no steadfast life;
but insofar as they never cease their constant interchange,
in this way they always are, unmoved in a cycle.
And Heraclitus too says that at one time the cosmos is turned to fire, at another it is composed again out of fire according to certain periods of time, in the words: "kindling in measures and being quenched in measures". Later the Stoics too were of this opinion. But let these be left aside;
